WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.125
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Your
mission-critical applications

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:04.300
depend on correct data.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.320
That's why developers rely on
relational databases, which

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.510
ensure data consistency.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.030
However, last
generation's databases

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.180
weren't designed to handle
today's scale and availability

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.590
requirements.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.570
In today's episode,
we're talking

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.660
about how Spanner provides the
highest level of consistency

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.740
of any distributed relational
database at unlimited scale.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.400
Consistency is one
of the main reasons

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.970
to choose a relational database
to back your applications.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.190
Strong consistency
guarantees ensure

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.050
that data is correct,
regardless of

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.780
how it's accessed or updated.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.300
Let's say you have a banking
system that transfers

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.160
money between accounts.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.990
Consistency ensures
that the debit

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.240
from one account and the
credit into the other either

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.940
both succeed or both fail.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.920
This simplifies
your applications

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.860
and lets your DBAs
sleep well at night.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.020
When it comes time
to scale, however,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.269
traditional relational
databases require

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.120
you to give up many of the
consistency protections

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.160
that made them attractive
in the first place.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.830
This is because they
rely on techniques

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.240
like sharding to divide the
data across multiple instances.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.060
The overhead required
to manage these shards

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.300
typically forces operators
to choose between performance

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.860
or consistency.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.350
In addition, highly
available systems

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.230
need to maintain multiple
copies of the data in case

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.450
one of the shards goes down.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.610
For many applications,
uncertainty

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.270
in the form of replication
lag or eventual consistency

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.230
is a recipe for complicated
code and tricky bugs.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.780
This is where Cloud
Spanner can help.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.870
Spanner is the only
distributed relational database

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.290
that provides external
consistency, the highest

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.330
consistency guarantee.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.400
This means that all
changes in the database

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.340
appear from the outside as
if they happen sequentially.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.090
This dramatically simplifies a
developer's view of the data.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:07.400
No more sharded queries, timing
bugs, or compensating logic.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.289
To your application,
Spanner looks

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.170
like a familiar
single-instance database.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.330
It handles the
complexities of ensuring

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.660
consistent transactions,
even as infrastructure scales

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.630
up or automatically fails over.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.140
Spanner is fully managed so
your operators don't ever

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.710
have to worry about
sharding or replication.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.590
So how does Spanner provide
external consistency

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.890
without sacrificing availability
or performance at scale?

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.650
Let's start by
imagining your database

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.230
resides on a single machine.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.810
In this case, it could
use a simple counter

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.550
to order incoming transactions.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:49.740
However, a single machine
can only scale up so far.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.140
Beyond that, you'll need
to distribute the work

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.120
across many machines.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.980
Keeping a single
counter would be

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.850
a bottleneck between hundreds
or thousands of servers.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.390
Spanner avoids this by
allowing each machine or node

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:08.130
to keep its own counter using a
clock, but not just any clock.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.120
Using a combination of GPS,
atomic clocks, and Google's

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.960
dedicated fiber optic network,
Spanner's TrueTime technology

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.660
ensures accurate
ordering of transactions

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.720
without expensive
centralized coordination.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.700
TrueTime simplifies
the work that Spanner

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.670
has to do to provide strict
external consistency.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.280
This streamlining translates
to better performance

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.010
and higher availability for
strong ACID transactions.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.730
Let's take a closer look at
how Spanner manages the data

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.650
involved in those transactions.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.810
Spanner partitions
each table's data

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.080
into independent
chunks called splits.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.250
Each split is assigned
internally to a server.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.530
Unlike traditional
sharding, these splits

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.530
are completely transparent
to downstream users.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.860
Spanner automatically optimizes
its split configurations

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.000
to balance out workloads
and avoid hotspots.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.800
When a split is
under heavy load,

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.400
for example, when updating the
inventory of a popular product,

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.550
Spanner resizes and
re-balances the affected splits

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.880
to avoid overloading
individual servers.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.280
Splitting and re-balancing
happen completely

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.040
behind the scenes,
dynamically adjusting based

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.100
on your data and workload.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.080
To avoid a single
point of failure,

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.910
each split in a
regional instance

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.510
is replicated across
three isolated zones.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.450
Spanner uses the well-known
Paxos consensus protocol

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.090
to ensure that everything
stays in sync, even

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.270
as the storage and
machines change underneath.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.550
During a write, Spanner
first determines

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.760
which split or splits
will be affected.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.830
Each split has a
leader among its copies

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:48.100
that coordinates
locking and replication.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.890
The leader assigns a timestamp
to the transaction using

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:55.570
TrueTime and tells the replicas
about the updates using Paxos.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.510
Once a majority of the replicas
acknowledge the update,

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.700
the leader directs them
to apply the changes

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.700
to the data at the
agreed-upon timestamp.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.310
Leadership updates dynamically
within an instance,

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.850
again, to avoid single
points of failure.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.160
For a query that doesn't
make any updates,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.170
Spanner avoids
the extra overhead

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.380
of taking locks, accessing
an always consistent copy

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.140
of the data at the
most recent timestamp.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.380
Combined, these optimizations
ensure consistency

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.080
and availability at scale.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.300
Spanner takes
distributed consistency

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:32.050
a step further with
multi-region configurations.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.300
Multi-region instances
replicate data

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.450
over two or three separate
geographic locations,

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:40.840
for example, the US,
Europe, and Asia.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.690
This added replication,
up to nine times,

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.620
provides industry-leading
five nines of availability.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.110
But doesn't all of this
come at a significant cost

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.730
to performance?

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.470
While, the underlying
work that Spanner

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.950
does to ensure consistency
and availability is not free.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.680
You only pay a performance
cost for what you actually use.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:05.110
In other words, if a query can
run without locking, it will.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.330
Or if a write only
touches one split,

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.080
it will bypass a more
expensive two-phase commit.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.490
This means that
your applications

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:15.010
will continue to
perform as they scale,

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.130
with all the same
consistency guarantees.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.380
By partitioning the
data into splits,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.510
Spanner can grow
beyond a single machine

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:25.870
and resolve queries in
parallel, improving scalability

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.970
and performance.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.970
By replicating splits over
multiple zones and regions

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.370
connected by Google's
dedicated network,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.920
Spanner is able to remain
highly available, up to 99.999%,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.560
even under the most
grueling workloads.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.640
Finally, by coordinating updates
using TrueTime and Paxos,

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.400
Spanner ensures that your
applications only ever see

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.400
a consistent view of the data.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:53.570
The best part?

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.180
All of this is handled
transparently for you

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.430
behind the scenes.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:00.560
So there you have
it, the magic sauce

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.360
behind Spanner's
consistency at scale.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.290
Thank you for joining us today
for this episode of "Spanner--

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:07.880
Database Unlimited."

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.230
